# Executive Summary and Business Recommendations

## The Problem

Every Bitcoin transaction is recorded publicly on the blockchain. But knowing
a transaction happened tells you nothing about who is behind it. Criminals
use this to their advantage, bouncing money through hundreds of wallets to
make dirty funds look clean before cashing out. Finding those transactions
manually is like searching for 900 specific people in a stadium of 200,000
without knowing what they look like.

## What This Model Does

This model reads Bitcoin transaction data and automatically identifies which
transactions are likely connected to crime. It was trained on real data
covering darknet drug markets, ransomware attacks, investment scams, and
Ponzi schemes. It learned the difference between how criminals move money
and how legitimate users transact.

## What It Found

Imagine a team of investigators sitting down to review 9,313 suspicious
transactions. Without this model, they read through all 9,313 to find the
909 that are actually criminal. That takes weeks.

With this model, they receive a shortlist of 799 transactions.
797 of those 799 are confirmed fraud.
They find nearly all the criminals in a fraction of the time,
and they almost never chase a lead that goes nowhere.

That is a 94% reduction in workload. Near zero false alarms.
99% overall accuracy. An AUC score of 0.997, where 1.0 is perfect.

## The Insight That Made It Work

The first version of this model looked only at transaction statistics,
amounts, fees, timing. It caught nothing. Zero fraud detected.

The breakthrough came from thinking about the network. Criminals do not
operate alone. They route money through chains of wallets, mixing services,
and intermediaries. A wallet connected to 161 other wallets behaves very
differently from one connected to 2. Once the model could see those
connections, it could see the crime.

## How to Use It

Feed transaction data into the model. It scores every transaction with a
fraud probability and flags the ones above the threshold for human review.
Investigators only see the flagged cases. Their time goes entirely toward
real investigations rather than sorting through noise.

The sensitivity can be adjusted without rebuilding anything. If the team
has capacity and wants to cast a wider net, lower the threshold and catch
more fraud with slightly more false alarms. If every investigator hour is
precious, raise it so that every single case on the desk is near certain.

## What to Do Next

Retrain the model every three to six months. Criminal methods evolve.
New mixing techniques emerge. A model that is not updated will gradually
become less effective as fraudsters adapt.

Invest in graph data infrastructure. The single biggest performance gain
in this project came from adding network connection data. More network
metrics mean better detection. The data exists on the blockchain. It just
needs to be extracted and fed into the model.

Plan for Phase 2. The current model looks at each transaction individually
plus its immediate connections. The next version, a Graph Neural Network,
will trace money flows across the entire network simultaneously, the way
a senior investigator follows a money trail across multiple accounts and
jurisdictions. That is where this technology is heading and where the
biggest gains remain.



